correct, because your code successfully handles edge cases and uses repeated division to verify that 3 is the only prime factor.

REVIEW MY CODE

What I did well: you handled the negative and zero edge cases right at the start. You also used a clean, easy to read while loop without making the logic overly complex.

Is it correct: yes, it is fully correct and passes all test cases.

Logical mistakes: there are no logical mistakes or missing edge cases in your code.

Time complexity: O(log n), specifically base 3, because you divide the number by 3 in every step of the loop, reducing the problem size exponentially.

Space complexity: O(1), because you only modify the input variable directly and use no extra memory.

Why my approach works: a power of three is made by multiplying 3 by itself some number of times. By dividing by 3 repeatedly, you strip away those 3s. If the number was a perfect power of three, you will always land exactly on 1.

ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Repeated Division (Your Approach)

Idea: keep dividing the number by 3 as long as there is no remainder. If you end up at 1, it is a power of three.

Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        while (n % 3 == 0) {
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(log n).

Space complexity: O(1).

What makes the next approach better: using math libraries can give us the answer without running a loop at all.

Approach 2: Mathematics using Logarithms

Idea: if a number is a power of 3, then its logarithm base 3 must be a whole integer. We can calculate this using base 10 logarithms and check if there are any decimal leftovers.

Code:

C++
#include <cmath>

class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        double result = log10(n) / log10(3);
        return fmod(result, 1.0) == 0;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(1) assuming the math library function runs in constant time.

Space complexity: O(1).

What makes the next approach better: floating-point math can sometimes have precision issues depending on the language or processor. We can avoid decimals entirely by looking at integer limits.

Approach 3: Math and Integer Limitations

Idea: a 32-bit signed integer has a maximum value of 2147483647. The largest power of 3 that fits inside this limit is 1162261467, which is 3 to the 19th power. Because 3 is a prime number, the only numbers that can perfectly divide 1162261467 are smaller powers of 3. So, we just check if 1162261467 divided by our number leaves a remainder of 0.

Code:

C++
class Solution {
public:
    bool isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        if (n <= 0) return false;
        return 1162261467 % n == 0;
    }
};
Time complexity: O(1) because it is a single math operation.

Space complexity: O(1).

WHERE I STAND

Which approach did I use: you used the repeated division approach (Approach 1).

Which approach does an interviewer expect: an interviewer expects your iterative loop approach first because it shows fundamental coding logic. However, this question is famous for its follow-up, where they will ask if you can solve it without any loops or recursion. When they ask that, they expect the integer limitation trick (Approach 3).

Common follow-up questions: Can you do it without a loop or recursion? Answer: Yes, by finding the largest power of 3 that fits in a standard integer and checking if it is perfectly divisible by the input number.

WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER

The key insight: a power of three has exactly one prime factor, which means it will always perfectly divide the maximum possible power of three that fits in memory.

